module examples.layout_runtime

# plans/M10.md item A2, 03-hardware.md §3.1: the fourth layout kind, laid
# out for real. It was declared-and-refused by item A (decision 561) until
# the field types it is *defined* in terms of existed; this case is the
# shape §3.1's own example spells, with the one difference item A2 could
# not close — the array length is a literal, not the `const N_TURNS` the
# doc writes (decision 580). Item A2b closed it, and
# `golden/check-layout-runtime-const-len` is §3.1's own spelling compiling;
# this case stays as the literal-length pin, which is the shape that must
# keep working with no `const` and no second pass involved at all.
#
# What the reported bytes show, and why each number is the only correct one:
#
# - `TurnArea` is 8 bytes, padding 0: two naturally-aligned `u32`s, exactly
#   the bytes its fields cover.
# - `turns: [TurnArea; 4]` is 32 bytes — `4 * 8`, elements back to back,
#   with no stride rounding and no trailing padding.
# - `turns` sits at 0x8, immediately after `rr_cursor`, because an array's
#   alignment is its *element's* (4), not its own size (32). A rule that
#   aligned a 32-byte field to 32 bytes would have invented 24 bytes of
#   padding here, which a `@layout` never does.
# - `rr_cursor` is `u64` and not `usize` (decision 563): a `@layout` type's
#   bytes are exact on every target, so no kind — including this one — gets
#   a target-dependent field. `err-layout-target-dependent` still pins that.
# - `GroupSlot` nests `TurnArea` *and* an array of it, so the recursive
#   size computation is visible in a second shape: 8 + 32 = 40 bytes.

@layout(runtime, endian=little)
struct TurnArea:
    state: u32
    waiter: u32


@layout(runtime, endian=little)
struct TurnTable:
    rr_cursor: u64
    turns: [TurnArea; 4]


@layout(runtime, endian=little)
struct GroupSlot:
    parent: TurnArea
    children: [TurnArea; 4]
